          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There are a number of approaches to apply
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability"} probability
            and related fields, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistics"} statistics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/information+theory"} information theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamical+systems"} dynamical systems
          %p
            On one hand, one can study the existing structures in traditional probability theory (such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+spaces"} probability spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            models the formation of spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
            and its iterations, used for example in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Finetti%27s+theorem"} de Finetti's theorem
          %p
            On the other hand, one can try to express certain aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability"} probability
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistics"} statistics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            which can be thought of as “fundamental” in probability and statistics, and which one can use to prove theorems, without having to use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            directly. One then proves that the usual measure-theoretic treatment is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+probability+theory"} synthetic probability theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+probability+theory"} synthetic probability theory
            is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+categories"} Markov categories
          %p The main end goals of categorical probability are
          %ul
            %li To generalize existing results in probability theory to more general settings, for example with less stringent conditions on countability, separability, etc.;
            %li To find new results, which with the traditional methods would have been too complex to prove;
            %li To make probability and related fields more accessible to practitioners, thanks to the fact that the formalism incorporates measure theory without requiring the user to have any deep knowledge of it.
          %h2#main_structures_of_interest Main structures of interest
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} Category theory
            was first developed to model particular structures in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
          %p
            The applications of category theory to probability are among the most recent, and are both bringing new categorical structures into the theory (such as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+categories"} Markov categories
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
          %h3#markov_categories Markov categories
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+categories"} Markov categories
            are a recent framework that models categories whose morphisms can be thought of as having randomness, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+maps"} stochastic maps
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernels"} Markov kernels
          %p It has a graphical formalism which keeps track of the stochastic dependencies, and which can be used to prove theorems in probability purely graphically.
          %p
            For more details, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category"} Markov category
          %h3#probability_monads Probability monads
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} Probability monads
            can be thought of as a way of adding a notion of “randomness” to an existing theory.
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            often models the idea of “forming spaces of particular structures”, and in probability theory, one is interested in forming spaces of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Finetti+theorem"} de Finetti
            situations one needs to form
            = succeed "." do
              %em probability measures over probability measures
          %p
            For more details, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monad"} probability monad
          %h3#dagger_categories Dagger categories
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+categories"} Dagger categories
            can be thought of as “undirected” categories, where morphisms can be seen as going either way as in an undirected graph.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transport+plans"} transport plans
            exhibit such a behavior, sometimes called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bayesian+inversion"} Bayesian inversion
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conditional+expectation"} conditional expectation
          %p
            For more details, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+couplings"} category of couplings
          %h2#main_results Main results
          %p Using category-theoretic methods, several results have been obtained in the past few years.
          %p
            Firstly, some known concepts and results of probability theory have been given a category-theoretic description. (For example: expressing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kolmogorov%27s+extension+theorem"} Kolmogorov's extension theorem
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofiltered+limit"} cofiltered limit
            condition.) This allows to incorporate the existing theory of probability into the categorical framework, and is the basic starting point for further results. (For example, every time in traditional probability Kolmogorov’s extension theorem is invoked, one now knows that a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            is being used.)
          %p Secondly, some classical results of probability theory have been restated and reproven using category theory. Often this adds new insight into the problem, and allows, for example, to drop further unnecessary assumptions (see the next point). In addition, the category-theoretic formalism often trades higher complexity for higher abstraction. This way, while more abstract, the categorical proofs tend to be simpler than their measure-theoretic counterparts. (And so, they also allow to prove more difficult results more easily.)
          %p
            Thirdly, and most importantly,
            %em new theorems
            have been proven, as well as generalizations and extensions of old theorems, especially from the discrete to the continuous case.
          %p Here is a partial list, roughly in alphabetical order, roughly divided by subject.
          %p (Work in progress, for more material see also the references below.)
